If you want to implement a "system dark mode" that follows the user's OS preference, you'll need to manipulate the `prefers-color-scheme` CSS property, which is supported in modern browsers. Here's how you'd do that:

```css
body {
    background-color: #000;
    color: #fff;
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
    transition: background-color 0.5s ease-in-out;
}

body[data-theme="dark"] {
    background-color: #000;
    color: #fff;
}

body[data-theme="light"] {
    background-color: #fff;
    color: #000;
}

/* Set the color scheme based on user OS preference */
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    body {
        --theme: dark;
        background-color: #000;
        color: #fff;
    }
}

@media (prefers-color-scheme: light) {
    body {
        --theme: light;
        background-color: #fff;
        color: #000;
    }
}
```

This way, if the user's OS prefers a dark mode, the `prefers-color-scheme: dark` media query will be applied, and your UI will automatically switch to dark mode.

To do that, you can use CSS. You can apply a single class to the body:

```css
body.light-mode {
  background-color: white;
  color: black;
}

body.dark-mode {
  background-color: black;
  color: white;
}
```

Then, add a class to your HTML element (or the body itself):

```html
<body class="dark-mode">
  <!-- your content here -->
</body>
```

If you'd rather have this automatically switch based on user preference, you can use user preference selectors:

```css
body {
  background-color: white;
  color: black;
}

body.dark-mode {
  background-color: black;
  color: white;
}

@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  body {
    background-color: black;
    color: white;
  }
}
```

This will automatically switch your UI to dark mode if your system prefers it.
